## 5. 领导力与行为面试题（Behavioral - STAR 原则）

### 5.1 通用行为问题

> **B1. Tell me about a time you mentored a junior or less experienced programmer.**
>
> "I paired a junior programmer on a lower-risk Level 3 output first, reviewed their self-check line by line with them rather than just returning comments, and explained the 'why' behind each standard convention. Within a few deliveries, they were independently producing Level 3 outputs with minimal peer-review findings."

> **B2. Describe a situation where you disagreed with a biostatistician on a derivation approach. How did you handle it?**
>
> "I raised the concern directly with the specific SAP section and a worked example showing how the two interpretations produced different denominators, rather than a general objection. In that case the statistician's interpretation was correct given a protocol amendment I hadn't accounted for, so I updated my derivation and documented the rationale for future reference."

> **B3. How do you handle a QC finding discovered close to a submission deadline?**
>
> "I flag it immediately to the study lead with a clear assessment of scope and risk, rather than trying to quietly fix it alone. Then I triage whether it's isolated to one output or systemic across multiple tables using the same derivation, so we fix and re-validate only what's actually affected."

> **B4. Why are you moving from an RWE/HEOR-heavy background into a more submission-focused Principal Statistical Programmer role, and how does your clinical (MD) background help?**
>
> "My clinical training means I don't just implement a derivation mechanically — I understand why a lab value might be missing, why an AE might be recorded a certain way, and where real clinical practice creates messy edge cases in the data. That's been valuable in both RWE and trial programming, because it helps me flag when a 'technically correct' derivation might not reflect what's clinically happening, before it becomes a finding in QC or in an FDA review."

### 5.2 STAR 作答结构提示

回答行为面试题时，建议严格按 **Situation → Task → Action → Result** 四步组织语言，每一步控制在 1-2 句：Situation/Task 交代背景不超过 20 秒，Action 是重点（说清楚"你"具体做了什么，而非团队做了什么），Result 尽量量化（如产出数量、发生率、时间节省）。

